From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SheepyLord <37598021+SheepyLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 05:15:31 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Let undrawn preloaded material textures give way under VRAM
 pressure

A USD material with preload_textures pins its textures at all their mips
from the moment the mod loads: requestMips forces every mip while
m_canDemote is false, and the budget pass and manageBudgetWithPriority skip
such textures. So every preloaded map of every character in the mods folder
stays whole for the whole session, drawn or not, and also after a USD
reload leaves no material referencing it. The budget, and patch 0025's
pressure cap, never reach them.

Such a texture now gives way under real pressure (patch 0025's limit
applies) once no instance has drawn it for 10 seconds: it streams like any
texture and drops to its smallest mip. Remix drops an instance one frame
after its last draw, so the wait keeps a character the camera turned away
from for a moment whole; textures never drawn go at once. As soon as an
instance draws it again it is pinned and loaded whole again, in the
background. While memory is free nothing changes. Only a material's own
preload yields: dome lights, textures too large for the streaming staging
buffer and rtx.texturemanager.neverDowngradeTextures stay as they were.

Patch 0025's limit is for all replacement textures, but the budget pass can
only shrink those that stream. Under pressure they now get the limit less
what pinned textures hold, so the overflow is taken from them and not left
to the driver.

The [RTX Texture Budget] line reports what pinned textures hold and counts
the releases and restores. Pure rules preloadPinChange and
streamedTextureLimitMib in rtx_texture_budget.h with checks in
test_texture_budget_pressure.

For tests, DXVK_TEXTURE_PRESSURE_TEST_BUDGET_MIB caps the budget this rule
sees (logged as a warning when set; the driver's own budget is unchanged):
on a large card WDDM pages another process's memory before it lowers a
foreground game's budget, so real pressure is hard to make there.

